Preface:

	Hey! Thank you for your time.

I decided to go for the Endless Walker Project (#5). Though, very quickly,
I ended up adding more and more features to make it more game-like:
A game that uses said walkers and more or less plays itself, while keeping track of its own records.


Prerequisites Implemented:

    - Playability, and replayability of an infinitely large field to walk on
    - Graphs for keeping track of in-game data (building up with repeats).
    - Obstacles which you cannot pass through, and portals transporting any walker that touches them to a random location on the field
    - Different, Settable methods of movement per each walker (Min-max step size, 3 different move styles: Free, Biased, Rigid)



Expansions Implemented:

4 EXTRA game modes:
	- Race: Game carries on until one walker reaches the goal and wins.
	- Coin Runners: Game carries on, and keeps spawning in coins (in accordance with
	    the rules .json file) until one runner collects the specified amount of coins and wins.
	- Survival: Don't hit the obstacles! If a walker hits one, he is instantly 	eliminated. Last one surviving wins. Spawns also scale with rules .json file
	- Cops n' Robbers: A selected number of walkers are selected as cops. They must
		catch the robbers by the dictated time limit!


Up to 5 separate walkers at once.
Visual GUI, Buttons and Lines to mark the walkers path
Music, and Sound Effects
Some additional Graphs (Distance from spawn point, extra-game-specific graphs...)


In addition to animated Walkers, obstacles and portals!
Using a BUNCH of saved images, together with an Animated Image class to dictate timings and positioning.
A lot is possible using PyGame...